ser said to use map or filter. However, sum can be used with map. But the user said to avoid loops, so using map is allowed.\n\nBut the user's guidelines say to use list comprehension. So perhaps the answer is to use a list comprehension for the digits, then sum.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\ndef sum_digits(n):\n    return sum(int(d) for d in str(n))\n\nBut the user said to avoid for loops. So using list comprehensions is acceptable.\n\nSo this should satisfy the guidelines. Let me check the steps again.\n\n1. Convert the number to a string: str(n).\n2. Map each character to int: map(int, str(n)).\n3. Sum the results.\n\nYes. So the function is correct. The list comprehension is used in the sum function, which is allowed.\n\nSo the solution is as above.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef sum_digits(n):\n    return sum(int(d) for d in str(n))\n</answer>"
