e user said to put the solution in experiments/solution.py. So the code will be in that file. The function is the only code in that file. So the code would be:\n\ndef multiply_list(nums):\n    product = 1\n    for num in nums:\n        product *= num\n    return product\n\nBut wait, the problem says the function should take a single list of integers. So the function is called multiply_list, and the input is a list. The code is straightforward.\n\nBut the user also mentioned that the code should be written as a linear script. So the code must be written in the order of the function, no other code. So the function is the only code in the file. Therefore, the code will be:\n\ndef multiply_list(nums):\n    product = 1\n    for num in nums:\n        product *= num\n    return product\n\nBut the user said to put the solution in experiments/solution.py. So the code will be in that file. The function is the only code. So the answer is that code.\n\nI need to make sure that the code is correct. Let\'s test with some examples. If nums is [2, 3, 4], product is 24. If nums is empty, product is 1. If nums is [5], product is 5. All correct.\n\nSo the final answer is the code as above.\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef multiply_list(nums):\n    product = 1\n    for num in nums:\n        product *= num\n    return product\n```'
